From 3e2c8e743108ad0762a38bcc8b963a51c8d9bf93 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: kamrul1157024 <kamrul1157024@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 12:32:59 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 7/8] fix(desktop): complete a typed path from the directory
 the chip names
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The picker completed against the filesystem but always from home: a path
with no leading `/` or `~` was resolved under `~/`, whatever the chip was
set to. So with the chip on `~/workspace/helios`, typing `int` offered
whatever home happens to hold — nothing, usually — and the one thing the
typing is for, walking down from where you already are, was the one thing
it could not do.

A relative path is now relative to the directory the picker is set to, the
way it is in a shell sitting in it. `completionTarget` takes that directory
as its base; without one it is home, which is where a session with no
directory starts anyway. `/…` and `~/…` say where they start from and are
untouched.

The same rule fixes the escape hatch, which was only half a hatch. It
committed the raw text, and `resolveCWD` refuses anything not absolute
(internal/server/cwd.go), so "anything typed is committable" held only for
the paths that did not need it: `Use "int"` produced a 400. `resolveTyped`
is what gets committed now, and the row prints the absolute path it will
land on, because three letters do not say which directory they are under.

The stub daemon grows two directories under /repo — a picker sitting in a
session's directory has to have something to complete — and stops matching
grep against directories, which it never should have.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
